Day 30: Address Book App (Phase 2 Review Project)

This is your Phase 2 milestone. No big new concepts except one: a dictionary inside a dictionary. Everything else is what you've already practiced (menus, dicts, validation, loops, search).

Problem Statement
Build an address book where each person's name maps to a dictionary of details:
address_book = {
    "Arun": {"phone": "9876543210", "email": "arun@gmail.com", "city": "Chennai"},
    "Priya": {"phone": "9123456780", "email": "priya@gmail.com", "city": "Madurai"}
}

Menu:
1. Add Contact
2. Search Contact
3. Update Contact
4. Delete Contact
5. View All Contacts
6. Exit
What each option should do
Add: ask for name, phone, email, city. If the name already exists, warn instead of overwriting.
Search: partial, case-insensitive match on the name (like your Day 21 search). Show all the details of each match.
Update: ask which field to change (phone / email / city), then the new value. The name must exist.
Delete: the name must exist. Ask for confirmation (y/n) before deleting.
View All: show every contact with all their details, nicely formatted.
New concept: nested dictionary
address_book = {}

# adding a person: the VALUE is another dictionary
address_book["Arun"] = {"phone": "9876543210", "email": "arun@gmail.com", "city": "Chennai"}

# reading one detail: two square brackets, outer key first, then inner key
print(address_book["Arun"]["phone"])     # → 9876543210

# updating one detail:
address_book["Arun"]["city"] = "Coimbatore"

# looping through everything:
for name in address_book:
    print(name)
    print("  Phone:", address_book[name]["phone"])
    print("  Email:", address_book[name]["email"])
    print("  City:", address_book[name]["city"])
Think of it like a cupboard. The outer dictionary is the cupboard, each person is a drawer (the name is the label), and inside each drawer there are three small boxes: phone, email, city.


